@** Introduction. UsdOptixViewer is a small, complete viewer for scenes
described in Pixar's Universal Scene Description (USD). It opens a stage, shows
its prims as a tree, lets the reader inspect every attribute, and draws the
geometry with a ray tracer that runs on NVIDIA's OptiX engine. The program was
written to exercise, in one place, a set of tools installed on a workstation:
CUDA and OptiX for ray tracing, SDL3 for the window and input, Dear ImGui for
the user interface, EnTT for the scene graph, and OpenUSD for reading scenes.
This edition, on the \texttt{cloudxr} branch of the repository, can also send
the view in stereo to a headset through OpenXR, and in particular through
NVIDIA CloudXR, which streams it over the network.

This document \emph{is} the program. Following Donald Knuth's \emph{Literate
Programming} (1984), the code is presented in the order in which it is best
understood by a human reader, not in the order a compiler wants it. Each piece
of code lives in a numbered \emph{section}, together with the prose that
explains it. A tool, \texttt{lp.py}, \emph{tangles} the sections back into the
seventeen source files of the program, and \emph{weaves} them into the book you
are reading.

@ The rendering is progressive. While the reader does not touch anything, the
viewer keeps adding one jittered sample per pixel and averages them, up to 256
samples. Materials follow the UsdPreviewSurface specification: diffuse colors
and textures, roughness and metallic values or maps, tangent-space normal maps,
alpha cutouts and per-face materials. Textures are filtered with mipmaps whose
level is chosen by ray differentials, and glossy reflections are sampled from
the GGX distribution of visible normals. All of these terms are explained where
they appear.

@* How to read the code. A \emph{chunk} of code has a name in angle brackets,
followed by the number of the section where it is first defined, as in
<<Install the viewer>>; the~$\equiv$ sign introduces its definition,
and~$\mathrel{+}\equiv$ marks a continuation of a chunk defined earlier. When a
chunk name appears inside another chunk, the code it names is to be inserted
at that point, indented like the reference. After each definition, a small note
tells where the chunk is used. A chunk whose name is a file name, such as
\lpfile{UsdOptixViewer/src/main.cpp}, is a \emph{root}: its expansion is
written to that file.

Code is typeset in the manner of Knuth's \textsc{cweb}: reserved words in
\textbf{bold}, identifiers in \textit{italic}, strings in
\texttt{typewriter} type and comments in roman. Some operators are shown with
their mathematical symbols: $\ne$ for \texttt{!=}, $\le$ and $\ge$ for
\texttt{<=} and \texttt{>=}, $\equiv$ for \texttt{==}, $\land$ and $\lor$ for
\texttt{\&\&} and \texttt{||}, $\neg$ for \texttt{!}, and $\rightarrow$ for
\texttt{->}. Long lines are broken and continued with a deeper indentation.

@* The parts of the program. The book follows the path of the data, from the
build system to the pixels on the screen:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{The build system} compiles the C++ code, turns the OptiX programs
  into PTX and embeds them in the executable.
\item \emph{Shared parameters} define the one structure that the CPU fills in
  and the GPU reads, \lpfile{LaunchParams.h}.
\item \emph{The scene} reads a USD stage, mirrors its prims into an EnTT
  registry, resolves materials and textures, and flattens all geometry into
  arrays of world-space triangles.
\item \emph{The renderer} creates the OptiX pipeline, builds the acceleration
  structure, uploads the textures with their mipmaps, and launches the ray
  tracing.
\item \emph{The device programs} run on the GPU: ray generation, shading,
  shadows, reflections and alpha cutouts.
\item \emph{The application} ties everything together with SDL3 and Dear ImGui:
  the hierarchy, the inspector, the viewport and the camera, and drives the XR
  output.
\item \emph{The XR output} talks to an OpenXR runtime through Vulkan, shares
  the eye images between Vulkan and CUDA, and can run the CloudXR runtime
  inside the program.
\item \emph{Installation for all users} shows the script that moved the tools
  to a system-wide location.
\end{itemize}
The third-party image decoder \lpfile{stb\_image.h}, about eight thousand lines
by Sean Barrett, is used as it comes and is not part of this book; nor is
NVIDIA's header \lpfile{cxrServiceAPI.h}, which declares the CloudXR service.

@* A word on correctness. Literate programs are easy to let drift away from the
code that actually runs. To prevent that, the command \texttt{python3 lp.py
check} tangles this book in memory and compares every root chunk, byte for
byte, with the corresponding file in the repository; it also fails if any
source file of the program has no root chunk. The book and the program
therefore always agree, and every line of the program appears somewhere in
these pages.
